| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Core concept: build a live network graph, monitor link utilization, detect congestion, recommend/reroute | Matches the well-documented, production-proven pattern (Hedera/Mahout-class systems) of detecting problematic flows and rerouting them specifically, rather than reinventing network management from scratch | Wasted throughput and undiagnosed bottlenecks from unmonitored network fabrics | Retains a proven, defensible mechanism as GreenCore's network-efficiency backbone | Low (sunk) | Keep as-is |
| Link-utilization monitoring as a core signal | A legitimate, necessary (if not sufficient alone) signal, consistent with standard network-observability practice | Basic visibility gap into fabric load | Retains the core sensing asset already planned | Low (sunk) | Keep, extend per FIX below |
| Recommend optimal traffic paths | Consistent with the detect-then-recommend pattern used by essentially every production system reviewed before any automatic action is taken | Lack of actionable guidance from raw monitoring data alone | Gives operators a concrete, explainable recommendation even before automation is trusted | Low | Keep |

## **FIX**

| **What** | **Why** | **Problem solved** | **Expected benefit** | **Cost/complexity** | **Priority** |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Add flow-level (elephant-flow) detection alongside link-utilization monitoring | Directly addresses the documented ECMP-collision blind spot that utilization-only monitoring cannot see; this is the specific, well-precedented problem Hedera/Mahout were built to solve | Undetected congestion from large-flow hash collisions that aggregate utilization does not reveal | Materially better congestion detection accuracy using a well-established, non-ML technique (periodic edge-switch flow polling) | Low-Medium (software, uses existing switch flow-statistics APIs) | MUST HAVE |
| Add dwell-time/hysteresis before triggering any reroute | The literature explicitly documents that reactive, undamped local rerouting can push congestion to neighboring links and trigger oscillating route recalculations, making the network worse, not better | Route-flapping / instability from over-eager automated rerouting | Prevents LightSpeed from becoming the source of the instability it was built to prevent | Low (parameter/policy only) | MUST HAVE |
| Add an explicit fail-safe-open controller/optimizer failure story | A centralized optimization layer is a documented single point of failure; without an explicit fallback the network could be worse off if LightSpeed's own layer fails | Risk of LightSpeed becoming a new point of fragility rather than a resilience improvement | Guarantees LightSpeed can never make connectivity worse than baseline BGP/ECMP, which is the strongest possible safety argument to a technical judge | Low-Medium (architectural discipline: keep the optimization layer additive/advisory to the underlying routing, not a replacement for it) | MUST HAVE |
| Scope automatic rerouting to a narrow, pre-validated case (detected elephant-flow collision on non-latency-sensitive traffic) | Matches exactly how Hedera/Mahout scope automatic action; a blanket "reroute on high utilization" policy is both riskier and less precise than the documented, proven pattern | Uncontrolled automation risk from acting on aggregate utilization alone | Removes the primary reason a pilot customer would refuse to enable automatic rerouting | Low (policy scoping, no new infrastructure) | MUST HAVE |

